WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.560
How the heck does that work?!

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.580
Hey Crazies.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.960
I’m adding a new kind of video into the
mix where I explain how things work.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.920
Today, we’re covering the Crookes Radiometer.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.660
What’s a Crookes Radiometer?

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.890
Great question!

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:24.980
It was invented by an English chemist and
physicist named William Crookes.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.600
A chemist and a physicist?!

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.940
Wow, that doesn’t happen a lot these days.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.240
Anyway, a Crookes radiometer looks something
like this.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.240
There are 4 vanes, each with a white side
and a black side.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.250
They’re enclosed in an air-tight glass bulb
where most of the air has been sucked out.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.540
When you shine light on it, the vanes spin.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.650
Simple enough to demonstrate, but
a huge pain to explain.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.720
There are 3 different effects at play:
pressure from light, also known as radiation

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.980
pressure, pressure from heated air, and air
vortices.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.700
Let’s look at those one at a time.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.560
Option 1: Radiation Pressure!

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.660
When atoms run into other atoms, they exchange
momentum.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.680
If there are a lot of atoms, we call that
pressure.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.240
Radiation is just a catch-all word for particles
that carry energy away.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.500
Any type of light counts as radiation.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.120
Photons have momentum too, which they can
also give to atoms after hitting them.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.799
If there are a lot of photons, we call that
radiation pressure.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.220
In the radiometer, the photons hit the white side
twice as hard as the black side,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.760
which gives us a net force in this direction.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.740
There’s a slight problem though.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.480
The momentum of a single photon is proportional
to its energy and, therefore, its frequency.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.780
That is a seriously tiny number!

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.940
When you consider in the dimensions of a radiometer
vane; the type of, strength of, and distance

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:48.980
to your light source; and finally multiply
by four; you get about 2.5 nano-newtons!

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.040
That’s itty bitty.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:55.639
So, while radiation pressure makes solar-sails
possible and balances the inward gravity of

00:01:55.639 --> 00:01:58.520
a star, those things are big.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.990
To get the tiny vanes in the radiometer to
respond you’d have to vent all the air to

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.760
space and shut off gravity to minimize friction.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.460
Physicslaaaaaaaaaaaaand!

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.420
I really need to figure out where that's coming
from.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.160
Anyway, I can do those things because I’m
on a spaceship!

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.080
But you can’t and the company that makes
these certainly can’t either.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.000
We know there’s more going on here for multiple
reasons.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:26.819
If radiation pressure was it, it would spin
this way, but a standard radiometer spins

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:27.819
the other way.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:29.760
It doesn’t even need to be light.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.420
Just getting it hot or cold will make it spin.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.760
Keep in mind, scientists have been discussing
this thing for over a century now.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.310
It started in the late 1800s between Maxwell
and Reynolds.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.380
They kept publishing papers as rebuttals to
each other, but then Maxwell died and the

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.140
Royal Society wouldn’t let Reynolds respond
anymore.

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:51.840
After that, no progress was made for almost
40 years because we just didn’t have the

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.980
technology to verify anything.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.320
Option 2: Pressure from heated air!

00:02:56.329 --> 00:03:00.430
A standard Crookes radiometer does have air
only a little bit, but it’s there.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.490
That air gets heated near the vanes, but more
so on the black side.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:09.200
The temperature increase causes a pressure
increase and that pressure pushes the vanes

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.200
forward.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.819
Is it really that simple?

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:12.900
No.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.900
This is the Science Asylum.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.120
You think we’re going to stay that superficial?

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.380
Temperature increases and pressure increases
don’t always correlate.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.469
We need to consider something called the Mean
Free Path.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:28.260
That’s the average distance a molecule can
travel before you expect to run into another

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.160
molecule.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.500
If the air is really thin, that could be the
size of the vanes in the radiometer.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:38.620
But, since the air molecules in the radiometer
are also hitting the vanes the pressure isn’t

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.840
evenly distributed across those vanes.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.800
Ricocheting molecules can keep other molecules
from hitting the vane.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.849
That happens more near the center than the
edge so the pressure across the vane looks

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:49.849
more like this.

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:53.329
It’s higher near the edges and less near
the center.

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:54.329
However!

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:58.920
The more air molecules there are in the glass
bulb, the smaller the mean free path.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.520
That does mean the pressure can distribute
across the vanes better but, too many, and

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:08.580
it’ll distribute throughout the whole radiometer
better resulting in effectively zero pressure

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.900
difference on the vanes.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.240
Option 3: Air Vortices!

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.400
Even when there isn’t a pressure difference
moving the vanes along that temperature difference

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.340
is still there.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.680
With more molecules around, that temperature
difference pushes the air like this and, by

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.560
Newton’s third law, the vanes are pushed
in the opposite direction.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.840
The radiometer vanes still spin.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.980
But as we mentioned before, the more air molecules
there are in the glass bulb, the smaller the

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.180
mean free path.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.560
If you put too many molecules in there, they
hit each other so much their motion becomes

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.560
random again.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.100
That’s why this doesn’t work at atmospheric
pressure.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.040
So which is it?

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.500
Option 2 or option 3?

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.440
Both!

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.460
Manufacturers of these radiometers have found
a sweet-spot at about 1 pascal.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.850
That’s about 10 millionths of an atmosphere,
so it’s pretty low.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.949
But it’s just the right number of air molecules,
so that both a pressure difference and an

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:00.880
air flow are at play inside the radiometer.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.100
Complicated!

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.580
So, crazies, what did you think?

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.470
